WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.120
Okay.

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:05.740
We now have the first major election forecast
for Biden versus Trump in November.

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:11.940
And it tells us exactly what I told you last
week, which is that Donald Trump can easily

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:13.710
win this election.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:19.510
I'm talking about the 538 forecast, which
sort of synthesizes all the polling information

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:23.690
at the national and state level, combines
it with historical data, combines it with

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:24.690
other factors.

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:29.960
It's all analyzed and outcomes, a number that
tells us how likely is it that Trump will

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:33.370
win and how likely is it that Joe Biden will
win.

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:37.320
And these numbers are then up to us to interpret.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:44.059
In this case, the numbers are that 538 gives
Joe Biden a 71 percent chance of winning.

00:00:44.059 --> 00:00:47.260
And it gives Donald Trump a 29 percent chance
of winning.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:48.260
Now.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:49.260
Careful.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:54.070
If you believe this is accurate, it is way
too close to think that anything is remotely

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:57.239
certain in this election, 71 to 29.

00:00:57.239 --> 00:00:59.899
In practical terms is 50 50 now.

00:00:59.899 --> 00:01:03.379
I want people to really understand the math
of this.

00:01:03.379 --> 00:01:08.780
What this means is that if you were to run
100 elections with today's inputs, today's

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:14.590
inputs in seventy one of those hundred elections,
Joe Biden would win.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:17.780
And in twenty nine of those hundred elections,
Donald Trump would win.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:18.860
If nothing changes.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:23.220
Now, of course, if anything changes between
now and November in the direction of Trump,

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:24.770
that 29 percent number goes up.

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:26.600
But forget about that.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.530
Twenty nine percent is really, really high.

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:31.410
I mean, just apply it to your life.

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:37.500
Does anybody get on a plane if there's a 29
percent chance of a problem with the plane's

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:38.500
flight worthiness?

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:39.980
Of course not.

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:44.720
Does anybody go to a dentist and you have
them fix a cavity?

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:49.420
If there is a 29 percent chance they make
a mistake, you would never go to that dentist

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:50.920
more trivially.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:56.830
I don't take a sip of water if I think there
is a two point nine percent chance the water

00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:58.020
is tainted.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:01:59.020
Never mind.

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:00.020
Twenty nine percent.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:03.720
So for all intents and purposes, in terms
of our behavior.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:06.100
Twenty nine percent is like 50/50.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:12.329
And if you need any more convincing about
that, when announcing the forecast, Nate Silver

00:02:12.329 --> 00:02:22.099
from 538 actually said, coincidentally, these
are the exact same odds as in our final forecast

00:02:22.099 --> 00:02:28.780
in 2016, which was Clinton 71 percent chance
to win, Trump 29 percent.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:32.730
Now, if you want to, do, you know, a little
bit of multiplication.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:38.989
The odds of Trump winning twice in a row with
29 percent likelihood is about eight percent.

00:02:38.989 --> 00:02:40.270
This is like flipping a coin.

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:44.010
If you flip a coin once, the odds of heads
are 50 percent.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:47.189
If after that first flip, you flip it a second
time.

00:02:47.189 --> 00:02:50.829
The odds of heads on that one flip are still
50 percent.

00:02:50.829 --> 00:02:55.000
But if in advance I say to you, what are the
odds of heads twice in a row?

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:00.200
You multiply point five times, point five,
and you get 25 percent.

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:05.550
Similarly, if Trump's odds really were twenty
nine percent in 2016 and they're 29 percent

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:12.790
now in the abstract, the odds of winning twice
with 29 percent odds are about eight percent.

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:14.450
But it doesn't matter.

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:16.849
It's 29 percent right now.

00:03:16.849 --> 00:03:20.930
It's essentially a coin flip from the standpoint
of how we should behave.

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:21.970
Just vote.

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:23.670
Guys, that's it.

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:29.510
And this reinforces, you know, when you see
Biden plus twelve, you might get the impression

00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:31.290
that we should be thinking about this differently.

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:32.290
It's 50/50.

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:33.540
This is a critical moment.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:35.060
This is not a rehearsal.

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:36.640
Let's not mess this up

